f 17592186429297 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049042 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429298 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049042 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429299 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049042 #edb/ref 1031 "The Area Between Curves Expressed as Functions of X" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049042 #edb/ref 1033 395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049042 #edb/ref 1034 1.2458505069580705 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049042 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "d575792a-9d1a-57df-86f8-ed13f8a5eb2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049885 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049885 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049885 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049885 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049885 #edb/ref 1031 "Volumes of Revolution Using the Washer Method: Rotation About Other Axes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049885 #edb/ref 1033 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049885 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "1b27fe0a-58ff-57f2-8e77-9341dd7fc153" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050548 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050548 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050548 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050548 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050548 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050548 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050548 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050548 #edb/ref 1031 "The Area Between Curves Expressed as Functions of Y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050548 #edb/ref 1033 402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050548 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "52acd6b1-a191-5ab4-a84a-969f6677fa4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115121 #edb/ref 1069 "e-589" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115121 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/70/70dc1594c0072b83429fc1f5d6be4fa454f6650c3eeef37c41c2d3ec691bb35f.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $y=x^2$ and the horizontal line $y=-1$ from $x=0$ to $x=1,$ as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $360^{\\circ}$about the line $y=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115121 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7d530adb-8bcc-53f1-b1bf-674be8f7046e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115121 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1$ along the $x$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/3b/3b0427e7d37245d86d347d6d4f867ae3d17e5f0a030feb53d28ad4bbc856f85c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x^2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\tV &= \\pi \\int_{0}^{1} \\big[\\: \\left({\\color{blue}\\textrm{upper function}}\\right) - \\left({\\color{brown}\\textrm{lower function}}\\right) \\:\\big]^2\\,\\textrm{d}x \\[5pt]\n\t \t&= \\pi \\int_{0}^{1} \\left[\\: {\\color{blue}x^2 }- \\left({\\color{brown}-1} \\right)\\:\\right]^2\\,\\textrm{d}x \\[5pt]  \n                &= \\pi\\int_{0}^{1} (x^2+1)^2 \\, \\textrm{d}x \\[5pt]\n\t\t&= \\pi\\int_{0}^{1} (x^4+2x^2+1) \\, \\textrm{d}x \\[5pt]\n\t\t&= \\pi \\left(\\dfrac{x^5}{5}+\\dfrac{2x^3}{3}+x \\right) \\Bigg|_{0}^{1} \\[5pt]\n\t\t&= \\pi \\left( \\left[ \\dfrac{1}{5}+\\dfrac{2}{3}+1 \\right] - 0 \\right) \\[5pt]\n\t\t&= \\dfrac{28 \\pi}{15}.\n}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115122 #edb/ref 1069 "e-590" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115122 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e9/e99f6cd10a72c91e5c7430a9ee18a45ba4dc09f941c779077567436a5fe90405.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x-1},$ the vertical line $x=1,$ and the horizontal line $y=1$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $x=1,$ as shown in the picture. Find an integral expression for the volume of the resulting solid." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115122 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a02ed650-9bbd-552b-9d80-c2d8f1d581b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115122 #edb/ref 1074 "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{x-1}\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx=y^3+1\n$$\n\n \n\nAlso, notice that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/73/73fc04f5a8a56b0faba6ec2a77f8aafd864c84786adfe7da9ccda99d0c8a4d8f.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=y^3+1.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\,({y}^{3}+1)-(1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{({y}^{3})}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{6}\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115126 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7148" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115126 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/40/401368d830825a55e27fc6ba191a1c3dbb80d93b2e0af74db3337523817d2c65.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=\\dfrac 3 2x+\\dfrac 1 2,$ for $x\\in [1,3],$ and the horizontal line $y=1$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $y=1,$ as shown above. Find an integral that represents the volume of the resulting solid." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115126 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "413e2100-cec8-5c20-981a-73b9a61ddbc4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115126 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that we are given that the limits of integration are from $x=1$ to $x=3$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/04/0434e16502f9b20bb53f3c46ba265239c59e8f5a487cdf3e71962fc0019a927d.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=\\dfrac 3 2x+\\dfrac 1 2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{[\\,(\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2})-(1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{(\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115127 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7149" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115127 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the region bounded by the curve $x=\\sqrt{y},$ the vertical line $x=2,$ and the horizontal line $y=4,$ as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $x=2.$\n\n![](images/a5/a56f30e5912629c9854e68d4837a37dae55a005e98ef6cf5379b5eb81022afb7.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115127 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4da6aa8a-844d-53c4-b573-2f81acb5a5c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115127 #edb/ref 1074 "We want to determine the limits of integration. To do so, we find the intersections of the curve and the line $x=2$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{y} & =2 \\\\ y & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=4$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/07/0748c87aa004f06f99a19ab4b7a2dadcf2f86920b53d1edd7ff19aced1d76f04.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=\\sqrt{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(2)-(\\sqrt{y})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[2-\\sqrt{y}]}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(4-4\\sqrt{y}+y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {[4y-\\frac{8}{3}{y}^{3/2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\pi [(4(4)-\\frac{8}{3}(4)^{3/2}+\\frac{(4)^{2}}{2})-(0)] \\\\  & =\\pi [16-\\frac{64}{3}+8] \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115280 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115280 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115280 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186115121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115280 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5c1cc6b6-5253-513c-98b0-9a963895eab6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115281 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115281 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115281 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115281 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115281 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115281 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115281 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921